<header><p class="st-kicker">nomadic confederation · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Yueban</h1><p class="st-meta">160 CE – 490 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A nomad kingdom north of Kucha formed by Northern Xiongnu who stayed in Central Asia after the Han broke their power.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After Han <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-xiongnu">Northern Xiongnu</a> in the late 1st century CE, part of them fled west. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-wei">Book of Wei</a>, those too weak to continue settled north of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kucha">Qiuci</a> (Kucha), between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tian-shan">Tian Shan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarbagatai-mountains">Tarbagatai</a>, forming a state called Yueban in the 2nd century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Chinese records describe a population of perhaps 200,000 who were cleaner in their habits than other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>, washing and cutting their hair, and who sent envoys to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-wei">Northern Wei</a> court in the 440s. Little else is known of its government or economy beyond its pastoral way of life.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Yueban was overrun by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/rouran-khaganate">Rouran Khaganate</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiele">Tiele</a> in the late 5th century, and its people were absorbed into neighbouring steppe groups.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/yueban.jpg" alt="Yueban" width="320" height="195"><figcaption>Image: Thomas A. Lessman, Talessman · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asia_400ad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>160 CE – 490 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> – <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>nomadic confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Xiongnu</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a>, <a href="/countries/kyrgyzstan">Kyrgyzstan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Yueban state</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=325&amp;focus=yueban" aria-label="Yueban on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6280 471 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Yueban lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7444 753h0M7402 767h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6600 727h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M6600 727h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Kazakhstan. <a href="/atlas?year=325&amp;focus=yueban">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
